I'd like to have an additional `BeginPlot()` method that enables me to force my own ID for a plot (I did not see another solution to achieve this). It's a minimal addition, I implemented it now as follows with an additional overload:
```
bool BeginPlot(const char* title_id, const ImVec2& size, ImPlot3DFlags flags) {
    IMPLOT3D_CHECK_CTX();

    // Skip if needed
    if (GImGui->CurrentWindow->SkipItems)
        return false;

    // Get or create plot
    return BeginPlot(GImGui->CurrentWindow->GetID(title_id), title_id, size, flags);
}

bool BeginPlot(const ImGuiID ID, const char* title_id, const ImVec2& size, ImPlot3DFlags flags) {
    IMPLOT3D_CHECK_CTX();
    ImPlot3DContext& gp = *GImPlot3D;
    IM_ASSERT_USER_ERROR(gp.CurrentPlot == nullptr, "Mismatched BeginPlot()/EndPlot()!");

    // Get window
    ImGuiContext& g = *GImGui;
    ImGuiWindow* window = g.CurrentWindow;

    // Skip if needed
    if (window->SkipItems)
        return false;

    // Get or create plot
//XXXXX    const ImGuiID ID = window->GetID(title_id);
```
The lower `BeginPlot()` is the original method with an additional first argument, this method does not retrieve the ImGui ID anymore. It could also be implemented with only an additional ID as last argument to the original method with default=0 (which may not be entirely bug-free...), test for that and decide if the ImGui ID should be used or the argument ID.

Maybe also better to typedef the `ImGuiID` argument to `ImPlot3dID' or so to avoid confusion...

I use this to save my plots to file: in order to get uniformly formatted PNGs I can now draw a plot from my GUI to a temporary window (which has a different ImGui ID than the normal plot ID so normally you're stuck there and you get a new and empty plot...). The temporary window always has the same size, I draw its plot in 'light' mode regardless the GUI skin, and then call some code to grab the RGB buffer and save it.

