@rubiktubik The issue turned out to be a type mismatch rather than the list logic itself.
envFrom expects a list of EnvFromSource structs, but the CEL expression was returning raw maps. Explicitly constructing the full struct (including optional fields like prefix and optional, set to null) resolves the validation error.

---

@rubiktubik ✅ Solution (Recommended): Move logic into the schema

Instead of building complex objects in the template, precompute envFrom in the schema and keep the template simple.

🔹 Schema
schema:
  apiVersion: v1alpha1
  kind: MyApplication
  spec:
    additionalEnv:
      configMapName: string | default=""
      secretName: string | default=""

🔹 Template (clean & readable)
envFrom: ${
  [
    schema.spec.additionalEnv.configMapName != "" ?
    {
      "configMapRef": {
        "name": schema.spec.additionalEnv.configMapName,
        "optional": null
      },
      "prefix": null
    } : null,

    schema.spec.additionalEnv.secretName != "" ?
    {
      "secretRef": {
        "name": schema.spec.additionalEnv.secretName,
        "optional": null
      },
      "prefix": null
    } : null
  ].filter(x, x != null)
}

✅ Why this is better

No list concatenation

Easier to read

Safer typing

Easier to extend later

I found a solution when combining both of your answers:

```yaml
envFrom: ${
                    (schema.spec.additionalEnv.configMapName != "" ?
                    [{
                    "configMapRef":{
                    "name":schema.spec.additionalEnv.configMapName,
                    "optional":false
                    },
                    "prefix":""
                    }] :[])
                    +
                    (schema.spec.additionalEnv.secretName != "" ?
                    [{
                    "secretRef":{
                    "name":schema.spec.additionalEnv.secretName,
                    "optional":false
                    },
                    "prefix":""
                    }]:[])
                    }
```

Combining two arrays and what is most important using `optional` and `prefix` to avoid type mismatch issues.

Thanks!

