I got confused because I can use `@message` inside component even for non-tracked props, so I assume that props changes in parent App component will be reactive in `useMessage` as it is reactive in Input component.

@trueadm 
**Question:**
Based on my exploration of ripple till now, I have 3 types of useful patterns when it comes to interacting and transporting reactivity of component props and I am not making all the props tracked input as I want to make sure tracked variable are only scoped and used inside the component where it defines.

Can we have compilation check that automatically transform code to reactive property as trackSplit(). Correct my if I am wrong, I feel that ripple reactivity is not user friendly for first time user as other framework and not easy to adopt without knowing pitfall when you want to explore advance cases or use pattern from another framework (in my case mostly react).

See below examples

- destruct the props inside the component function definition. (in this reactivity lost)

 ```jsx
component Input({ message, ...props }) {
 const newMessage = useMessage({ message });
 <input value={@newMessage} {...props}/>
}
```

- destruct the props using `trackSplit()` - (in this reactivity works)
 ```jsx
component Input1(props){
  const [message, rest] = trackSplit(props, ['message']);
  const newMessage = useMessage({ message });
  <input value={@newMessage} {...@rest} />
}
```

> Note: I rarely pass the whole props to other function as code is not that readable.  (in this reactivity works only when I use prop.message inside useMessage())
```jsx

function useMessage(prop){
  return track(() => {
    return prop.@message
  })
}

component Input(props) {
 const newMessage = useMessage(props); //<-- not that clear which props useMessage() uses without looking useMessage()
 <input value={@newMessage} />
}
```

- use tracked props as input for component, here I have mixed uses of message variable both `message` and `@message` which I wanted to avoid.
```jsx

component Parent(){
  const message = track(() => 'Hello, World!');
  <Input {message} />
  <button onClick={() => @message = 'Bye, World!'}>{"Send"}</button>
}

component Input({ message, ...props }){
  const newMessage = useMessage({ message });
  <input value={@newMessage} />
}
```

